| Interpolation is the first thing in the language that turns a number back
| into characters, so both runtimes have to agree on the spelling exactly —
| the interpreter through Rust's `Display`, the compiled program through
| `runtime.c`'s `text_push_number`. This fixture is the cross-check: the
| harness runs it in both modes, so any drift fails here rather than
| silently producing two different strings.
|
| The rule is the shortest decimal that reads back as the same double, laid
| out positionally (never in exponent form).
let whole = 42
let neg = -7
let half = 2.5

assert "$whole" = "42"
assert "$neg" = "-7"
assert "$half" = "2.5"

| Not 0.3: the sum really is a hair above it, and the shortest decimal that
| round-trips has to say so. Rounding this to "0.3" was a live bug during
| implementation.
let sum = 0.1 + 0.2
assert "$sum" = "0.30000000000000004"

| A repeating fraction stops at the last digit that still round-trips.
let third = 1 / 3
assert "$third" = "0.3333333333333333"

| Small magnitudes spell out their leading zeros rather than switching to
| an exponent.
let tiny = 0.0000001
assert "$tiny" = "0.0000001"

| Negative zero keeps its sign, which the integral fast path would otherwise
| drop on the way through a C `long long`.
let negzero = -0
assert "$negzero" = "-0"
